var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"search/#Searching-Earthdata","page":"Search","text":"EarthData.jl searches CMR granule and collection endpoints and returns typed UMM records:\n\nusing EarthData\n\ngranule_results = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\ncollection_results = collections(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\n\nThe accepted keyword arguments are CMR search parameters. Use fieldnames(EarthData.GranuleRequest), fieldnames(EarthData.CollectionRequest), and fieldnames(EarthData.QueryParams) to inspect the supported names.","title":"Searching Earthdata"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Granules","page":"Search","text":"granules returns Vector{EarthData.Granules.UMM_G}. Each record exposes the UMM-G fields from NASA's schema as Julia struct fields:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\nfirst(gg).GranuleUR\nfirst(gg).RelatedUrls\n\nBy default, EarthData.jl requests one CMR page. Set page_size to control the page size and all=true to continue through all pages using the CMR-Search-After response header:\n\nall_granules = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", page_size=2000, all=true)","title":"Granules"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Spatial-subsetting","page":"Search","text":"Granule searches accept CMR's spatial parameters — bounding_box, point, line, circle and polygon:\n\ngg = granules(\n    short_name=\"MCD43A3\",\n    version=\"061\",\n    bounding_box=\"-51.0,66.0,-49.0,68.0\",\n)\n\nThese are not interchangeable. A bounding_box's edges follow parallels and meridians, while a polygon's edges are great-circle arcs, so away from the equator a polygon through the same four corners selects a smaller area.","title":"Spatial subsetting"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Geometries","page":"Search","text":"The same parameters also accept an Extents.Extent or any GeoInterface geometry, so a geometry you already have can be used directly:\n\nusing Extents\n\ngg = granules(\n    short_name=\"MCD43A3\",\n    version=\"061\",\n    bounding_box=Extent(X=(-51.0, -49.0), Y=(66.0, 68.0)),\n)\n\nEach parameter takes the geometry that matches it:\n\nparameter geometry\nbounding_box Extent, or anything with an extent\npoint point, or (lon, lat)\nline line, line string\npolygon polygon, linear ring\ncircle (lon, lat, radius_m), (point, radius)\n\nCMR needs a polygon closed and wound counter-clockwise; both are handled for you, since CMR reads a clockwise ring as everything outside it. CMR's polygon is a single ring, so a polygon with holes is rejected rather than quietly searching the filled area.\n\nPassing a vector of geometries searches their union, which is how CMR reads a repeated spatial parameter:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"MCD43A3\", version=\"061\", point=[(-50.0, 67.0), (-40.0, 60.0)])\n\nStrings are still passed to CMR unchanged.","title":"Geometries"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Dates","page":"Search","text":"The date-valued parameters — temporal among them — take a Date or DateTime as well as the ISO 8601 string CMR expects, so the formatting is handled for you:\n\nusing Dates\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", temporal=Date(2019, 4, 18))\n\nA tuple is a range, and either side may be nothing for an open bound:\n\ngranules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", temporal=(Date(2019, 4, 18), Date(2019, 5, 1)))\ngranules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", temporal=(Date(2019, 4, 18), nothing))   # everything since\ngranules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", temporal=(nothing, Date(2019, 5, 1)))    # everything until\n\nA Date is taken as midnight UTC and a DateTime as already being UTC, since CMR has no way to read a local offset. Strings are passed through unchanged.","title":"Dates"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Coverage-of-a-result","page":"Search","text":"A granule or collection states its own spatial coverage, and Extents.extent reads it back:\n\nusing Extents\n\ng = first(granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\"))\nExtents.extent(g)  # Extent(X = (-105.27, -103.82), Y = (-2.09, -0.01))\n\nThe UMM geometry types implement GeoInterface, so a result's coverage is a geometry like any other — it can go straight into GeometryOps, Rasters.crop, or another search:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"MCD43A3\", version=\"061\", polygon=first(g.SpatialExtent.HorizontalSpatialDomain.Geometry.GPolygons))\n\nAn extent goes straight to bounding_box, so coverage computed elsewhere needs no conversion:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"MCD43A3\", version=\"061\", bounding_box=Extents.extent(raster))\n\nBoundingRectangleType(extent) builds the UMM record itself, for when a rectangle is what you want rather than a search parameter.","title":"Coverage of a result"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Collections","page":"Search","text":"collections searches collection metadata and returns Vector{EarthData.Collections.UMM_C}:\n\ncc = collections(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\nfirst(cc).EntryTitle\nfirst(cc).Abstract","title":"Collections"},{"category":"section","location":"search/#Request-method","page":"Search","text":"CMR searches use POST by default. Use method=:GET when compatibility with a GET-only proxy or workflow is required:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", method=:GET)","title":"Request method"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Downloads-and-URLs","page":"Downloads","text":"Search results usually contain several related URLs. EarthData.jl provides helpers for extracting HTTPS and S3 URLs from UMM records and for downloading individual files or batches of files.","title":"Downloads and URLs"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#URL-helpers","page":"Downloads","text":"Use urls to collect every related URL, or filter by scheme with https_urls, s3_urls, or download_url:\n\njulia> struct ExampleItem <: EarthData.AbstractJSON\n           RelatedUrls::Vector{NamedTuple{(:URL,), Tuple{String}}}\n       end\n\njulia> item = ExampleItem([(URL = \"https://example.test/G1.h5\",), (URL = \"s3://example-bucket/G1.h5\",)]);\n\njulia> https_urls(item)\n1-element Vector{String}:\n \"https://example.test/G1.h5\"\n\njulia> s3_urls(item)\n1-element Vector{String}:\n \"s3://example-bucket/G1.h5\"\n\njulia> download_url(item)\n\"https://example.test/G1.h5\"\n\nThe same helpers also accept vectors of UMM records:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\nhttps_urls(gg)\ns3_urls(gg)","title":"URL helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Selecting-the-data-file","page":"Downloads","text":"A record's related URLs are not all data. A single MCD43A3 granule, for example, carries eight: the HTTPS file, its S3 copy, a DOI landing page, a metadata sidecar, browse imagery and a cloud-credentials endpoint. https_urls returns most of those, so filter on RelatedUrls[].Type instead — data_urls does exactly that, keeping only \"GET DATA\":\n\ndata_urls(first(gg))          # the file\ndownload(gg, \"data\"; type=\"GET DATA\")\n\nThe S3 copy of the same file is typed \"GET DATA VIA DIRECT ACCESS\":\n\nurls(first(gg); scheme=:s3, type=\"GET DATA VIA DIRECT ACCESS\")","title":"Selecting the data file"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Granule-sizes","page":"Downloads","text":"granule_size reports a granule's size in bytes:\n\ngranule_size(first(gg))\n\nIt prefers SizeInBytes, and otherwise converts Size using the record's SizeUnit. Size alone is unit-less — providers commonly report megabytes — so reading it without the unit understates the size by a factor of about a million.","title":"Granule sizes"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Earthdata-credentials","page":"Downloads","text":"NASA Earthdata downloads require credentials. Store them in your .netrc file with netrc!:\n\nEarthData.netrc!(\"earthdata_username\", \"earthdata_password\")\n\nThe file is plaintext, so use the same care you would use for any credential file.","title":"Earthdata credentials"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Bearer-tokens","page":"Downloads","text":"Earthdata Login also issues bearer tokens, which is the credential an HTTP client needs when it sets its own headers rather than delegating to curl's .netrc support. EarthData.token() resolves one from ENV[\"EARTHDATA_TOKEN\"], else the first non-comment line of ~/.edl_token, and auth_headers builds the header:\n\nheaders = EarthData.auth_headers()          # Authorization: Bearer <token>\nDownloads.download(data_urls(first(gg))[1], \"granule.h5\"; headers)\n\nResolution never touches the network. To obtain a token from your .netrc credentials instead, call EarthData.token_from_netrc() explicitly:\n\nENV[\"EARTHDATA_TOKEN\"] = EarthData.token_from_netrc()\n\nwarning: Two concurrent tokens, maximum\nAn account may hold two live tokens; requesting a third returns HTTP 403. token_from_netrc() lists existing tokens and returns the first, so it cannot exhaust the quota; token_from_netrc(create=true) will consume a slot when the account has none. Never call either from a retry loop. Tokens last 60 days.","title":"Bearer tokens"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Downloading-files","page":"Downloads","text":"Download one URL to a path:\n\ngg = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\ndownload(download_url(first(gg)), \"GEDI02_A_example.h5\")\n\nDownload many HTTPS URLs into a folder. EarthData.jl uses aria2c by default for batch HTTPS downloads:\n\ndownload(gg, \"data\")\n\nTo write a URL list for another tool:\n\nwrite_urls(\"urls.txt\", gg)","title":"Downloading files"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#Errors-and-retries","page":"Downloads","text":"Not every failure means the same thing, so EarthData.jl sorts them into two groups:\n\nTemporary — 5xx, 408, 429, and connection faults. The service said nothing about the request, so waiting and trying again can work. These throw TransientError.\nPermanent — 400, 401, 403, 404. These throw an ArgumentError saying what to fix. A 403 from a DAAC nearly always means the account has not accepted the collection's licence, and retrying cannot help.\n\nGetting this wrong is costly either way: one brief 502 should not end an hours-long run, and a 403 should not be retried until the deadline.\n\nThe split is by status, not by exception type. Downloads.download raises Downloads.RequestError both for a transport fault and for any HTTP error status, so a 403 and a 503 arrive as the same type and only the status tells them apart.\n\nwith_retries applies the split, backing off exponentially up to a minute. A Retry-After from the server takes priority, capped at five minutes. Pass deadline (an absolute time()) to bound the whole thing — the attempt cap is set high enough to wait out a maintenance window, so deadline is what should normally stop a run.\n\nEarthData.with_retries(context=\"my download\") do\n    download(url, path)\nend\n\nSearches are classified through the existing requester hook:\n\ngranules(short_name=\"MCD43A3\", version=\"061\", requester=classifying_requester())\n\nWithout it a CMR 503 arrives as a generic ErrorException from parse_cmr_error, which looks the same as a rejected query.","title":"Errors and retries"},{"category":"section","location":"downloads/#S3-downloads","page":"Downloads","text":"S3 support is provided by the optional AWSS3 extension. After loading AWSS3, EarthData.jl can request temporary Earthdata Cloud S3 credentials and download S3 URLs:\n\nusing AWSS3\n\nconfig = EarthData.create_aws_config(\"nsidc\", \"us-west-2\")\nEarthData.s3download(\"s3://bucket/path/file.h5\", \"file.h5\", config)","title":"S3 downloads"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#API-reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Public-API","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#UMM-G-schema-types","page":"Reference","text":"Granule search results are parsed as EarthData.Granules.UMM_G and related schema types generated from NASA's UMM-G schema.","title":"UMM-G schema types"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#UMM-C-schema-types","page":"Reference","text":"Collection search results are parsed as EarthData.Collections.UMM_C and related schema types generated from NASA's UMM-C schema.","title":"UMM-C schema types"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.auth_headers-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"auth_headers(; bearer=EarthData.token()) -> Vector{Pair{String,String}}\n\nAuthorization: Bearer header for an Earthdata Login token, for clients that set their own headers rather than relying on .netrc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.auth_headers"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.check_response-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"check_response(r, context)\n\nThrow for an error response: TransientError if retrying could help, an ArgumentError saying what to fix if it cannot. Returns nothing otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.check_response"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#EarthData.classifying_requester","page":"Reference","text":"classifying_requester(inner=HTTP.request, context=\"CMR search\")\n\nWrap a requester (as accepted by request, granules and collections) so a temporary status throws TransientError instead of being turned into a plain ErrorException by parse_cmr_error.\n\nOnly temporary statuses are intercepted. The rest are passed through untouched, so parse_cmr_error's reading of CMR's errors array is still what the caller sees.\n\ngranules(short_name=\"MCD43A3\", version=\"061\", requester=classifying_requester())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.classifying_requester"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.cmr_spatial-Tuple{Symbol, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"cmr_spatial(param::Symbol, value)\n\nConvert value into the string CMR expects for the spatial parameter param. Strings and nothing pass through; geometries are converted; anything else raises an ArgumentError naming what param accepts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.cmr_spatial"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.cmr_temporal-Tuple{Symbol, AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"cmr_temporal(param::Symbol, value)\n\nConvert value into the string CMR expects for the temporal parameter param. Strings and nothing pass through; dates are converted; anything else raises an ArgumentError naming what param accepts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.cmr_temporal"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.collections-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"collections(; keyword=value, ...) -> Vector{Collections.UMM_C}\n\nSearch for collections using NASA EarthData Search. Possible keywords are (:concept_id, :doi, :echo_collection_id, :provider_short_name, :entry_title, :entry_id, :archive_center, :data_center, :temporal, :project, :consortium, :updated_since, :created_at, :has_granules_created_at, :has_granules_revised_at, :revision_date, :processing_level_id, :platform, :instrument, :sensor, :spatial_keyword, :science_keywords, :two_d_coordinate_system_name, :collection_data_type, :granule_data_format, :online_only, :downloadable, :browsable, :keyword, :provider, :native_id, :short_name, :version, :tag_key, :variable_name, :variable_native_id, :variable_concept_id, :variables, :service_name, :service_type, :service_concept_id, :tool_name, :tool_type, :tool_concept_id, :polygon, :bounding_box, :point, :line, :circle, :attribute, :author, :has_granules, :has_granules_or_cwic, :has_granules_or_opensearch, :has_opendap_url, :cloud_hosted, :standard_product, :sort_key, :all_revisions) or (:page_size, :page_num, :offset, :scroll, :sort_key, :pretty, :token, :echo_compatible).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.collections"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.data_urls-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"data_urls(item; scheme=:https) -> Vector{String}\n\nURLs of the granule's data file(s), i.e. the related URLs typed \"GET DATA\".\n\nRecords commonly carry several related URLs that are not the data: a DOI landing page, a metadata sidecar, browse imagery, a cloud-credentials endpoint. Filtering on the scheme alone does not separate those from the file, so prefer this over https_urls when what you want is something to download. Note the S3 copy of the same file is typed \"GET DATA VIA DIRECT ACCESS\" and so is not returned; use urls(item; scheme=:s3, type=\"GET DATA VIA DIRECT ACCESS\") for that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.data_urls"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.error_status-Tuple{Downloads.RequestError}","page":"Reference","text":"error_status(err) -> Int\n\nThe HTTP status err carries, or 0 when it carries none.\n\nThe exception type alone does not say whether a failure was temporary. Downloads.RequestError is thrown for both kinds: a transport fault carries a libcurl code with response.status == 0, while a plain HTTP error status carries code == CURLE_OK and the status in response.status. A 503 worth retrying and a 403 that never will be are the same type, so the status has to be read rather than inferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.error_status"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.granule_size-Tuple{EarthData.AbstractJSON}","page":"Reference","text":"granule_size(granule; default_unit=\"MB\") -> Union{Int,Nothing}\n\nSize of a granule in bytes, from DataGranule.ArchiveAndDistributionInformation, or nothing when the record does not report one.\n\nSizeInBytes is used when present. Otherwise Size is converted using its SizeUnit — Size is unit-less on its own, and providers do report units other than bytes, so reading the number without the unit is how a megabyte figure gets mistaken for a byte count. A record that gives Size with no SizeUnit is read as default_unit, which is what the DAACs mean in practice; pass default_unit explicitly if a provider differs. A SizeUnit of \"NA\" is not a unit, so such an entry is skipped rather than guessed at.\n\nSizes of multiple files are summed, since together they are the granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.granule_size"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.granules-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"granules(; keyword=value, ...) -> Vector{Granules.UMM_G}\n\nSearch for granules using NASA EarthDataSearch. Possible keywords are (:collection_concept_id, :granule_ur, :readable_granule_name, :online_only, :downloadable, :browsable, :attribute, :polygon, :bounding_box, :point, :line, :circle, :equator_crossing_longitude, :equator_crossing_date, :updated_since, :revision_date, :created_at, :production_date, :cloud_cover, :platform, :instrument, :sensor, :project, :concept_id, :echo_granule_id, :echo_collection_id, :day_night_flag, :two_d_coordinate_system, :provider, :native_id, :short_name, :version, :entry_title, :entry_id, :temporal, :cycle, :passes, :sort_key) or (:page_size, :page_num, :offset, :scroll, :sort_key, :pretty, :token, :echo_compatible).\n\ng = first(granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\"))\nstartswith(sprint(show, g), \"GEDI02_A: \")\n# output\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.granules"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.https_urls-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"https_urls(item) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe item's related URLs that use the https scheme.\n\nEvery related URL is returned, not just the data: a DOI landing page, a metadata sidecar and browse imagery all use https too. See data_urls when what you want is something to download.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.https_urls"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.is_transient-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"is_transient(err) -> Bool\n\nWhether err is worth retrying: a TransientError or a connection failure. Deliberately narrow — an ArgumentError (permanent status, bad query) and a plain ErrorException are not.\n\nWhen err carries an HTTP error status (error_status) that status decides, so a 401/403/404 surfacing as a Downloads.RequestError is permanent — matching check_response — rather than transient by virtue of its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.is_transient"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.is_transient_status-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Reference","text":"is_transient_status(status) -> Bool\n\nWhether an HTTP status is worth retrying: a 5xx, a 408 or a 429.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.is_transient_status"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.netrc!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"netrc!(username, password)\n\nWrites/updates a .netrc file for ICESat-2 and GEDI downloads. A .netrc is a plaintext file containing your username and password for NASA EarthData and DAACs, and can be automatically used by Julia using Downloads and tools like wget, curl among others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.netrc!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#EarthData.netrc_credentials","page":"Reference","text":"netrc_credentials(machine=\"urs.earthdata.nasa.gov\"; path=nothing) -> (login, password)\n\nRead login and password for machine from .netrc, the counterpart to netrc!.\n\n.netrc is a whitespace-separated token stream, so the tokens are scanned in order and the values following the target machine are taken until the next machine or default entry. The macdef form is not supported. path defaults to ENV[\"NETRC\"], else ~/.netrc (~/_netrc on Windows).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.netrc_credentials"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.retry_after_seconds-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"retry_after_seconds(response) -> Float64\n\nSeconds the server asked us to wait, from Retry-After, or 0.0 when the header is absent or unparseable. Only the delta-seconds form is read; clamped to retry_after_max.\n\nReads response.headers directly rather than going through HTTP.header, which only accepts an HTTP.Messages.Message — a Downloads.Response is a plain struct, and that is the shape /s3credentials fails with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.retry_after_seconds"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.ring_coords-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"ring_coords(ring) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nFlatten a ring for CMR's polygon, which needs it closed and wound counter-clockwise. Both are fixed here rather than asked of the caller: a clockwise ring is a perfectly valid geometry, but CMR reads it as the complement, i.e. the whole globe except the intended area, giving a wrong answer rather than an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.ring_coords"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.s3_urls-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"s3_urls(item) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe item's related URLs that use the s3 scheme, for direct in-region S3 access.\n\nReading these requires DAAC-issued temporary credentials and an AWSS3 session; see create_aws_config. As with https_urls, the filter is on the scheme alone, so the result is not necessarily just the data file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.s3_urls"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.size_unit_factor-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Reference","text":"size_unit_factor(unit) -> Union{Int,Nothing}\n\nBytes per unit, for the SizeUnit values UMM allows (\"Bytes\", \"KB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"; binary multiples). Throws for an unrecognised unit rather than guessing, since a wrong factor silently misreports a size by three orders of magnitude.\n\n\"NA\" returns nothing: providers use it to say they recorded no unit, so the accompanying Size cannot be converted at all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.size_unit_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.token-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"token() -> String\n\nThe Earthdata Login bearer token, from the environment or a token file.\n\nSearches, in order:\n\nENV[\"EARTHDATA_TOKEN\"]\n~/.edl_token (first non-empty, non-comment line)\n\nThrows an ErrorException with setup instructions when neither is present.\n\nThis never contacts the network. To obtain a token from .netrc credentials instead, call token_from_netrc explicitly.\n\nEARTHDATA_TOKEN is a community convention rather than a NASA-defined variable; no NASA tooling reads it for you.\n\nTreat the returned string as a secret: it authenticates as the account for 60 days.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.token"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.token_from_netrc-Tuple{}","page":"Reference","text":"token_from_netrc(; create=false, machine=\"urs.earthdata.nasa.gov\") -> String\n\nFetch an Earthdata Login bearer token using the username and password stored in .netrc.\n\nThis is a network call, kept out of token on purpose: a credential lookup that can hit the network is a lookup that can hang or rate-limit inside a retry loop.\n\nwarning: Two concurrent tokens, maximum\nEDL allows an account two live tokens; requesting a third returns HTTP 403. With create=false (the default) this only lists tokens and returns the first, so it cannot exhaust the quota. create=true will consume the second slot when none exists. Do not call this from a retry loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.token_from_netrc"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.urls-Tuple{EarthData.AbstractJSON}","page":"Reference","text":"urls(item; scheme=nothing, type=nothing) -> Vector{String}\n\nRelated URLs of a UMM record, optionally filtered by URL scheme (:https, :s3) and by RelatedUrls[].Type (\"GET DATA\", \"GET DATA VIA DIRECT ACCESS\", \"VIEW RELATED INFORMATION\", ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.urls"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.with_retries-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reference","text":"with_retries(f; context, attempts=retry_attempts, verbose=true, deadline=Inf) -> f()\n\nCall f, retrying while it fails temporarily (see is_transient) with exponential backoff from retry_base up to retry_max_backoff. Other errors propagate on the first attempt, and the last failure is rethrown as-is so the user sees the service's own message.\n\ndeadline is an absolute time() bounding the retrying, and it — not attempts — is what should normally stop a run. A Retry-After from the server overrides the backoff upward.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.with_retries"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Extents.extent-Tuple{Union{EarthData.Collections.UMM_C, EarthData.Granules.UMM_G}}","page":"Reference","text":"Extents.extent(record::Union{Granules.UMM_G,Collections.UMM_C}) -> Union{Extent,Nothing}\n\nThe record's spatial extent in degrees, or nothing when it states no coverage.\n\nX/Y enclose every geometry the record carries — CMR states coverage as bounding rectangles, polygons, points or lines, and a record may hold more than one kind.\n\nExtents is not re-exported, so reach it either with using Extents or, as here, through EarthData.Extents.\n\ng = first(granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\"))\next = EarthData.Extents.extent(g);\nhaskey(ext, :X) && haskey(ext, :Y)\n# output\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Extents.extent"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.CollectionRequest","page":"Reference","text":"CollectionRequest(; keyword=value, ...)\n\nAs documented by https://cmr.earthdata.nasa.gov/search/site/docs/search/api.html#collection-search-by-parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.CollectionRequest"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.GranuleRequest","page":"Reference","text":"GranuleRequest(; keyword=value, ...)\n\nAs documented by https://cmr.earthdata.nasa.gov/search/site/docs/search/api.html#granule-search-by-parameters. See fieldnames(GranuleRequest) for a list of all possible keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.GranuleRequest"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.BoundingRectangleType-Tuple{Extents.Extent}","page":"Reference","text":"BoundingRectangleType(extent::Extents.Extent)\n\nA UMM bounding rectangle covering extent, which needs X and Y.\n\nThis is how an extent from elsewhere reaches a search: Extents.extent(raster) gives X/Y that CMR cannot read, and a rectangle is a geometry granules accepts.\n\nextent = EarthData.Extents.Extent(X=(-51.0, -49.0), Y=(40.0, 60.0))\nrect = EarthData.Granules.BoundingRectangleType(extent)\nrect.WestBoundingCoordinate\n# output\n-51.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.BoundingRectangleType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.System","page":"Reference","text":"System(; cmr_url, edl_host)\n\nAn Earthdata deployment: PROD or UAT.\n\nUAT is where NASA stages a collection before it goes public, so it is what a provider tests against. Pass one as system to granules or collections.\n\ncmr_url is a base URL rather than a host, so a proxy or a local CMR can be reached by constructing a System directly. edl_host is a bare hostname, since that is what .netrc and curl match on.\n\nnote: Search only, for now\nNothing reads edl_host yet — token_from_netrc and netrc_credentials still target production. A UAT search works; UAT authentication does not, and a production token rejected by UAT reports a production token page.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.System"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.TransientError","page":"Reference","text":"TransientError(context, status, detail, retry_after=0.0)\n\nA response that says nothing about the request: a 5xx, a 408 or a 429. Worth retrying, unlike a 401/403/404.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.TransientError"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#EarthData.PROD","page":"Reference","text":"PROD\n\nThe operational Earthdata deployment, and the default for every search.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.PROD"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#EarthData.UAT","page":"Reference","text":"UAT\n\nEarthdata's user-acceptance-testing deployment, holding staged and pre-release collections.\n\ngranules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\", system=EarthData.UAT)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.UAT"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.AccessConstraintsType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about any physical constraints for accessing the data set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.AccessConstraintsType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.AdditionalAttributeType","page":"Reference","text":"A reference to an additional attribute in the parent collection. The attribute reference may contain a granule specific value that will override the value in the parent collection for this granule. An attribute with the  same name must exist in the parent collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.AdditionalAttributeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.BoundaryType","page":"Reference","text":"A boundary is set of points connected by straight lines representing a polygon on the earth. It takes a minimum of three points to make a boundary. Points must be specified in counter-clockwise order and closed (the first and last vertices are the same).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.BoundaryType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.BoundingRectangleType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity holds the horizontal spatial coverage of a bounding box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.BoundingRectangleType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.CharacteristicType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity is used to reference characteristics defined in the parent collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.CharacteristicType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.ChecksumType","page":"Reference","text":"Allows the provider to provide a checksum value and checksum algorithm name to allow the user to calculate the checksum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.ChecksumType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.CollectionReferenceType","page":"Reference","text":"A reference to a collection metadata record's short name and version, or entry title to which this granule metadata record belongs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.CollectionReferenceType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.DataGranuleType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity stores the basic descriptive characteristics associated with a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.DataGranuleType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.ExclusiveZoneType","page":"Reference","text":"Contains the excluded boundaries from the GPolygon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.ExclusiveZoneType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.FilePackageType","page":"Reference","text":"This set of elements describes a file package or a file that contains other files.  Normally this is either a tar or a zip file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.FilePackageType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.FileType","page":"Reference","text":"This set of elements describes a file. The file can be a part of the entire granule or is the granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.FileType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.GPolygonType","page":"Reference","text":"A GPolygon specifies an area on the earth represented by a main boundary with optional boundaries for regions excluded from the main boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.GPolygonType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.GeometryType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity holds the geometry representing the spatial coverage information of a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.GeometryType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.HorizontalSpatialDomainType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about a granule with horizontal spatial coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.HorizontalSpatialDomainType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.IdentifierType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity stores an identifier. If the identifier is part of the enumeration then use it. If the enumeration is 'Other',  the provider must specify the identifier's name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.IdentifierType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.InstrumentType","page":"Reference","text":"A reference to the device in the parent collection that was used to measure or record data, including direct human observation. In cases where instruments have a single composed of child instrument (sensor) or the instrument and composed of child instrument (sensor) are used synonymously (e.g. AVHRR) the both Instrument and composed of child instrument should be recorded. The child instrument information is represented by child entities. The instrument reference may contain granule specific characteristics and operation modes. These characteristics and modes are not checked against the referenced instrument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.InstrumentType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.LineType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity holds the horizontal spatial coverage of a line. A line area contains at lease two points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.LineType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.MeasuredParameterType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity contains the name of the geophysical parameter expressed in the data as well as associated quality flags and quality statistics. The quality statistics element contains measures of quality for the granule. The parameters used to set these measures are not preset and will be determined by the data producer. Each set of measures can occur many times either for the granule as a whole or for individual parameters. The quality flags contain the science, operational and automatic quality flags which indicate the overall quality assurance levels of specific parameter values within a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.MeasuredParameterType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.MetadataSpecificationType","page":"Reference","text":"This object requires any metadata record that is validated by this schema to provide information about the schema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.MetadataSpecificationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.OrbitCalculatedSpatialDomainType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity is used to store the characteristics of the orbit calculated spatial domain to include the model name, orbit number, start and stop orbit number, equator crossing date and time, and equator crossing longitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.OrbitCalculatedSpatialDomainType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.OrbitType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity stores orbital coverage information of the granule. This coverage is an alternative way of expressing granule spatial coverage. This information supports orbital backtrack searching on a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.OrbitType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.PGEVersionClassType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity stores basic descriptive characteristics related to the Product Generation Executable associated with a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.PGEVersionClassType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.PlatformType","page":"Reference","text":"A reference to a platform in the parent collection that is associated with the acquisition of the granule. The platform must exist in the parent collection. For example, Platform types may include (but are not limited to): ADEOS-II, AEM-2, Terra, Aqua, Aura, BALLOONS, BUOYS, C-130, DEM, DMSP-F1,etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.PlatformType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.PointType","page":"Reference","text":"The longitude and latitude values of a spatially referenced point in degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.PointType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.ProjectType","page":"Reference","text":"Information describing the scientific endeavor with which the granule is associated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.ProjectType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.ProviderDateType","page":"Reference","text":"Specifies the date and its type that the provider uses for the granule. For Create, Update, and Insert the date is the date that the granule file is created, updated, or inserted into the provider database by the provider. Delete is the date that the CMR should delete the granule metadata record from its repository.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.ProviderDateType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.QAFlagsType","page":"Reference","text":"The quality flags contain the science, operational and automatic quality flags which indicate the overall quality assurance levels of specific parameter values within a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.QAFlagsType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.QAStatsType","page":"Reference","text":"The quality statistics element contains measures of quality for the granule. The parameters used to set these measures are not preset and will be determined by the data producer. Each set of measures can occur many times either for the granule as a whole or for individual parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.QAStatsType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.RangeDateTimeType","page":"Reference","text":"Stores the data acquisition start and end date/time for a granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.RangeDateTimeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.RelatedUrlType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity holds all types of online URL associated with the granule such as guide document or ordering site etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.RelatedUrlType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.SpatialExtentType","page":"Reference","text":"This class contains attributes which describe the spatial extent of a granule. Spatial Extent includes any or all of Granule Localities, Horizontal Spatial Domain, and Vertical Spatial Domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.SpatialExtentType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.TemporalExtentType","page":"Reference","text":"Information which describes the temporal extent of a specific granule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.TemporalExtentType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.TilingCoordinateType","page":"Reference","text":"Defines the minimum and maximum value for one dimension of a two dimensional coordinate system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.TilingCoordinateType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.TilingIdentificationSystemType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity stores the tiling identification system for the granule. The tiling identification system information is an alternative way to express granule's spatial coverage based on a certain two dimensional coordinate system defined by the providers. The name must match the name in the parent collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.TilingIdentificationSystemType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.TrackPassTileType","page":"Reference","text":"This element stores a track pass and its tile information. It will allow a user to search by pass number and their tiles that are contained with in a cycle number.  While trying to keep this generic for all to use, this comes from a SWOT requirement where a pass represents a 1/2 orbit. This element will then hold a list of 1/2 orbits and their tiles that together represent the granules spatial extent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.TrackPassTileType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.TrackType","page":"Reference","text":"This element stores track information of the granule. Track information is used to allow a user to search for granules whose spatial extent is based on an orbital cycle, pass, and tile mapping. Though it is derived from the SWOT mission requirements, it is intended that this element type be generic enough so that other missions can make use of it. While track information is a type of spatial domain, it is expected that the metadata provider will provide geometry information that matches the spatial extent of the track information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.TrackType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Granules.VerticalSpatialDomainType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity contains the type and value for the granule's vertical spatial domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Granules.VerticalSpatialDomainType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.AccessConstraintsType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about any constraints for accessing the data set. This includes any special restrictions, legal prerequisites, limitations and/or warnings on obtaining the data set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.AccessConstraintsType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.AdditionalAttributeType","page":"Reference","text":"Additional unique attributes of the collection, beyond those defined in the UMM model, which the data provider deems useful for end-user understanding of the data in the collection.  Additional attributes are also called Product Specific Attributes (PSAs) or non-core attributes.  Examples are HORIZONTALTILENUMBER, VERTICALTILENUMBER.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.AdditionalAttributeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.AddressType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity contains the physical address details for the contact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.AddressType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.AltitudeSystemDefinitionType","page":"Reference","text":"The reference frame or system from which altitude is measured. The term 'altitude' is used instead of the common term 'elevation' to conform to the terminology in Federal Information Processing Standards 70-1 and 173. The information contains the datum name, distance units and encoding method, which provide the definition for the system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.AltitudeSystemDefinitionType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ArchiveAndDistributionInformationType","page":"Reference","text":"This element and all of its sub elements exist for display purposes. It allows a data provider to provide archive and distribution information up front to an end user, to help them decide if they can use the product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ArchiveAndDistributionInformationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.AssociatedDoiType","page":"Reference","text":"This element stores the DOI (Digital Object Identifier) that identifies the collection. Note: The values should start with the directory indicator which in ESDIS' case is 10.  If the DOI was registered through ESDIS, the beginning of the string should be 10.5067. The DOI URL is not stored here; it should be stored as a RelatedURL. The DOI organization that is responsible for creating the DOI is described in the Authority element. For ESDIS records the value of https://doi.org/ should be used. NASA metadata providers are strongly encouraged to include DOI and DOI Authority for their collections using CollectionDOI property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.AssociatedDoiType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.BoundaryType","page":"Reference","text":"A boundary is set of points connected by straight lines representing a polygon on the earth. It takes a minimum of three points to make a boundary. Points must be specified in counter-clockwise order and closed (the first and last vertices are the same).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.BoundaryType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.CharacteristicType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity is used to define characteristics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.CharacteristicType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ContactInformationType","page":"Reference","text":"Defines the contact information of a data center or data contact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ContactInformationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ContactMechanismType","page":"Reference","text":"Method for contacting the data contact. A contact can be available via phone, email, Facebook, or Twitter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ContactMechanismType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.DataCenterType","page":"Reference","text":"Defines a data center which is either an organization or institution responsible for distributing, archiving, or processing the data, etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.DataCenterType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.DateType","page":"Reference","text":"Specifies the date and its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.DateType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.DepthSystemDefinitionType","page":"Reference","text":"The reference frame or system from which depth is measured. The information contains the datum name, distance units and encoding method, which provide the definition for the system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.DepthSystemDefinitionType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.DirectDistributionInformationType","page":"Reference","text":"This element allows end users to get direct access to data products that are stored in the Amazon Web Service (AWS) S3 buckets. The sub elements include S3 credentials end point and a documentation URL as well as bucket prefix names and an AWS region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.DirectDistributionInformationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.DirectoryNameType","page":"Reference","text":"Formerly called Internal Directory Name (IDN) Node (IDN_Node). This element has been used historically by the GCMD internally to identify association, responsibility and/or ownership of the dataset, service or supplemental information. Note: This field only occurs in the DIF. When a DIF record is retrieved in the ECHO10 or ISO 19115 formats, this element will not be translated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.DirectoryNameType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.DoiDoiType","page":"Reference","text":"This element stores the DOI (Digital Object Identifier) that identifies the collection. Note: The values should start with the directory indicator which in ESDIS' case is 10.  If the DOI was registered through ESDIS, the beginning of the string should be 10.5067. The DOI URL is not stored here; it should be stored as a RelatedURL. The DOI organization that is responsible for creating the DOI is described in the Authority element. For ESDIS records the value of https://doi.org/ should be used. NASA metadata providers are strongly encouraged to include DOI and DOI Authority for their collections using CollectionDOI property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.DoiDoiType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ExclusiveZoneType","page":"Reference","text":"Contains the excluded boundaries from the GPolygon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ExclusiveZoneType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.FootprintType","page":"Reference","text":"The largest width of an instrument's footprint as measured on the Earths surface. The largest Footprint takes the place of SwathWidth in the Orbit Backtrack Algorithm if SwathWidth does not exist. The optional description element allows the user of the record to be able to distinguish between the different footprints of an instrument if it has more than 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.FootprintType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.GeodeticModelType","page":"Reference","text":"This element describes the geodetic model for the data product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.GeodeticModelType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.GetDataType","page":"Reference","text":"Represents the information needed for a DistributionURL where data is retrieved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.GetDataType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.GetServiceType","page":"Reference","text":"Represents a Service through a URL where the service will act on data and return the result to the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.GetServiceType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataGenericResolutionType","page":"Reference","text":"Generic Resolutions object describes general resolution data for a data product where it is not known if a data product is gridded or not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataGenericResolutionType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionGriddedRangeType","page":"Reference","text":"Gridded Range Resolutions object describes range resolution data for gridded data products.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionGriddedRangeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionGriddedType","page":"Reference","text":"Gridded Resolutions object describes resolution data for gridded data products.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionGriddedType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionNonGriddedRangeType","page":"Reference","text":"Non Gridded Range Resolutions object describes range resolution data for non gridded data products.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionNonGriddedRangeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionNonGriddedType","page":"Reference","text":"Non Gridded Resolutions object describes resolution data for non gridded data products.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionNonGriddedType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionType","page":"Reference","text":"This class defines a number of the data products horizontal data resolution. The horizontal data resolution is defined as the smallest horizontal distance between successive elements of data in a dataset. This is synonymous with terms such as ground sample distance, sample spacing and pixel size. It is to be noted that the horizontal data resolution could be different in the two horizontal dimensions. Also, it is different from the spatial resolution of an instrument, which is the minimum distance between points that an instrument can see as distinct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalDataResolutionType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.HorizontalSpatialDomainType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about a collection with horizontal spatial coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.HorizontalSpatialDomainType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.InstrumentChildType","page":"Reference","text":"Child object on an instrument. Has all the same fields as instrument, minus the list of child instruments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.InstrumentChildType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.InstrumentType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about the device used to measure or record data in this collection, including direct human observation. In cases where instruments have a single child instrument or the instrument and child instrument are used synonymously (e.g. AVHRR), both Instrument and ComposedOf should be recorded. The child instrument information is represented in a separate section. The controlled vocabulary for instrument names is maintained in the Keyword Management System (KMS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.InstrumentType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.LocationKeywordType","page":"Reference","text":"This element defines a hierarchical location list. It replaces SpatialKeywords. The controlled vocabulary for location keywords is maintained in the Keyword Management System (KMS). Each tier must have data in the tier above it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.LocationKeywordType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.MetadataAssociationType","page":"Reference","text":"Used to identify other services, collections, visualizations, granules, and other metadata types and resources that are associated with or dependent on this collection, including parent-child relationships.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.MetadataAssociationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.MetadataSpecificationType","page":"Reference","text":"This object requires any metadata record that is validated by this schema to provide information about the schema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.MetadataSpecificationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.OnlineResourceType","page":"Reference","text":"Describes the online resource pertaining to the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.OnlineResourceType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.OrbitParametersType","page":"Reference","text":"Orbit parameters for the collection used by the Orbital Backtrack Algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.OrbitParametersType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.PaleoTemporalCoverageType","page":"Reference","text":"For paleoclimate or geologic data, PaleoTemporalCoverage is the length of time represented by the data collected. PaleoTemporalCoverage should be used when the data spans time frames earlier than yyyy-mm-dd = 0001-01-01.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.PaleoTemporalCoverageType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.PeriodicDateTimeType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about Periodic Date Time collections, including the name of the temporal period in addition to the start and end dates, duration unit and value, and cycle duration unit and value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.PeriodicDateTimeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.PlatformType","page":"Reference","text":"Describes the relevant platforms used to acquire the data in the collection. The controlled vocabularies for platform types and names are maintained in the Keyword Management System (KMS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.PlatformType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.PointType","page":"Reference","text":"The longitude and latitude values of a spatially referenced point in degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.PointType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ProcessingLevelType","page":"Reference","text":"This element contains the Processing Level Id and the Processing Level Description\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ProcessingLevelType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ProjectType","page":"Reference","text":"Information describing the scientific endeavor(s) with which the collection is associated. Scientific endeavors include campaigns, projects, interdisciplinary science investigations, missions, field experiments, etc. The controlled vocabularies for project names are maintained in the Keyword Management System (KMS)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ProjectType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.PublicationReferenceType","page":"Reference","text":"Describes key bibliographic citations pertaining to the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.PublicationReferenceType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.RangeDateTimeType","page":"Reference","text":"Stores the start and end date/time of a collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.RangeDateTimeType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.RelatedUrlType","page":"Reference","text":"Represents Internet sites that contain information related to the data, as well as related Internet sites such as project home pages, related data archives/servers, metadata extensions, online software packages, web mapping services, and calibration/validation data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.RelatedUrlType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ResolutionAndCoordinateSystemType","page":"Reference","text":"This class defines the horizontal spatial extents coordinate system and the data product's horizontal data resolution. The horizontal data resolution is defined as the smallest horizontal distance between successive elements of data in a dataset. This is synonymous with terms such as ground sample distance, sample spacing and pixel size. It is to be noted that the horizontal data resolution could be different in the two horizontal dimensions. Also, it is different from the spatial resolution of an instrument, which is the minimum distance between points that an instrument can see as distinct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ResolutionAndCoordinateSystemType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ResourceCitationType","page":"Reference","text":"Building block text fields used to construct the recommended language for citing the collection in professional scientific literature.  The citation language constructed from these fields references the collection itself, and is not designed for listing bibliographic references of scientific research articles arising from search results. A list of references related to the research results should be in the Publication Reference element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ResourceCitationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.ScienceKeywordType","page":"Reference","text":"Enables specification of Earth science keywords related to the collection.  The controlled vocabulary for Science Keywords is maintained in the Keyword Management System (KMS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.ScienceKeywordType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.SpatialExtentType","page":"Reference","text":"Specifies the geographic and vertical (altitude, depth) coverage of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.SpatialExtentType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.SpatialInformationType","page":"Reference","text":"This entity stores the reference frame or system from which horizontal and vertical spatial domains are measured. The horizontal reference frame includes a Geodetic Model, Geographic Coordinates, and Local Coordinates. The Vertical reference frame includes altitudes (elevations) and depths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.SpatialInformationType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.TemporalExtentType","page":"Reference","text":"Information which describes the temporal range or extent of a specific collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.TemporalExtentType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.TilingCoordinateType","page":"Reference","text":"Defines the minimum and maximum value for one dimension of a two dimensional coordinate system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.TilingCoordinateType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.TilingIdentificationSystemType","page":"Reference","text":"Information about a two-dimensional tiling system related to this collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.TilingIdentificationSystemType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.UseConstraintsDescriptionType","page":"Reference","text":"This sub-element either contains a license summary or free-text description that details the permitted use or limitation of this collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.UseConstraintsDescriptionType"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#EarthData.Collections.UseConstraintsType","page":"Reference","text":"This element defines how the data may or may not be used after access is granted to assure the protection of privacy or intellectual property. This includes license text, license URL, or any special restrictions, legal prerequisites, terms and conditions, and/or limitations on using the data set. Data providers may request acknowledgement of the data from users and claim no responsibility for quality and completeness of data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EarthData.Collections.UseConstraintsType"},{"category":"section","location":"#EarthData","page":"Home","text":"(Image: Build Status) (Image: Coverage)\n\nEarthData.jl is a Julia interface to NASA's Common Metadata Repository (CMR), the metadata service behind Earthdata Search. It searches granules and collections, parses UMM JSON responses into Julia structs, extracts HTTPS and S3 download URLs, and downloads data with Earthdata credential handling.","title":"EarthData"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"] add EarthData","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"julia> using EarthData\n\n# Search for GEDI L2A granules\njulia> gg = granules(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\n10-element Vector{EarthData.Granules.UMM_G}:\n GEDI02_A: GEDI02_A_2019108002012_O01959_01_T03909_02_003_01_V002\n GEDI02_A: GEDI02_A_2019108002012_O01959_02_T03909_02_003_01_V002\n GEDI02_A: GEDI02_A_2019108002012_O01959_03_T03909_02_003_01_V002\n ...\n\n# A single granule shows its collection short name and granule id\njulia> gg[1]\nGEDI02_A: GEDI02_A_2019108002012_O01959_01_T03909_02_003_01_V002\n\njulia> gg[1].RelatedUrls[1].URL\n\"https://e4ftl01.cr.usgs.gov//GEDI_L1_L2/GEDI/GEDI02_A.002/2019.04.18/GEDI02_A_2019108002012_O01959_01_T03909_02_003_01_V002.h5\"\n\n# Search collections\njulia> cc = collections(short_name=\"GEDI02_A\")\n\n# Extract HTTPS or S3 URLs from UMM search results\njulia> https_urls(gg[1])\n\njulia> s3_urls(gg[1])\n\n# Download one URL using Earthdata .netrc/cookie handling\njulia> download(download_url(gg[1]), \"GEDI02_A_example.h5\")\n\n# Download many HTTPS URLs with aria2c\njulia> download(gg, \"data\")\n\n\nSee the search and download pages for pagination, collection search, URL helpers, batch downloads, and optional S3 access.\n\nPages = [\"search.md\", \"downloads.md\", \"reference.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Quick start"}]
}
